WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.960
A small training sample may have working
day mornings and no nonworking day at

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.410
two.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.230
The pair two, zero is valid,
but its group mean is undefined.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.890
Our declared fallback is the global
mean of recorded training targets.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.650
We choose this before evaluation
and save it with the model.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.070
Validation answers never
fill the missing group.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.570
An invalid request is different.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.490
Hour twenty four or a working day
value of two must raise an error.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.060
Treating malformed input as a missing
group would conceal a problem in the

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.230
request or schema.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.570
A fallback handles limited training
coverage, not arbitrary invalid values.

00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:46.183
Here is the complete training fixture.

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.543
The pair eight,
one has targets ten and thirty.

00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:54.303
Its group mean is twenty
and its support is two.

00:00:54.973 --> 00:00:58.323
The pair two, one has one target of two.

00:00:58.893 --> 00:01:02.263
The pair eight,
zero has one target of one hundred.

00:01:02.953 --> 00:01:07.743
Those groups have means two and one
hundred, each supported by one row.

00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:12.323
We have four recorded training
rows across three observed groups.

00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:15.223
The pair two, zero is absent.

00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:20.633
Keeping the counts matters because a group
with two rows represents twice as many

00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:25.063
recorded hours as a one row group
when we calculate the global fallback.

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:29.203
Add the four training targets.

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:35.163
Ten plus thirty plus two plus one
hundred gives one hundred and forty two.

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:41.263
Divide by the four recorded training
rows to obtain thirty five point five.

00:01:42.173 --> 00:01:47.178
Averaging the three group means equally
would instead give about forty point six

00:01:47.178 --> 00:01:47.783
seven.

00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:51.663
That assigns equal weight to
groups with different support.

00:01:52.093 --> 00:01:55.983
Our fallback estimates the
level across recorded hours,

00:01:56.393 --> 00:01:59.103
so the original row
weights must be preserved.

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:03.773
A different fallback hierarchy,
such as an hour only mean,

00:02:03.853 --> 00:02:07.363
would be a different modelling
policy to declare and compare.

00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:11.463
It is not introduced silently
when a difficult request appears.

00:02:13.608 --> 00:02:17.178
The executable example fits
those four training rows.

00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:21.998
Requesting eight, one returns the
observed group's mean of twenty.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:27.818
Requesting two, zero returns the global
fallback of thirty five point five.

00:02:28.008 --> 00:02:30.948
Change the first requested key to eight,

00:02:30.948 --> 00:02:35.358
zero and its answer becomes one hundred,
because that group exists.

00:02:36.128 --> 00:02:38.938
The absent key still returns
thirty five point five.

00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:42.698
Neither call creates a new
group or uses an outcome.

00:02:43.488 --> 00:02:46.458
Repeating the missing lookup
leaves the model unchanged.

00:02:47.348 --> 00:02:52.008
The test also changes the original input
lists after fitting and verifies that the

00:02:52.008 --> 00:02:53.578
saved model is unaffected.

00:02:54.128 --> 00:02:56.238
Its state contains the fitted statistics,

00:02:56.768 --> 00:02:59.058
not references to mutable training lists.

00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:06.687
The real training year contains all forty
eight valid hour and working day pairs.

00:03:07.257 --> 00:03:11.577
The model therefore uses no fallback
for its actual training or validation

00:03:11.577 --> 00:03:12.467
predictions.

00:03:13.217 --> 00:03:15.827
Those zero counts are
saved in the run receipt.

00:03:16.657 --> 00:03:20.527
The real score establishes the
performance of observed group lookup.

00:03:21.097 --> 00:03:25.307
The small fixture establishes what
happens for a valid absent group.

00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:30.497
A branch never used by a successful
dataset still needs a meaningful known

00:03:30.497 --> 00:03:31.527
answer check.

00:03:31.917 --> 00:03:34.387
If we later made the groups more specific,

00:03:34.617 --> 00:03:37.027
coverage and support would
need fresh inspection.

00:03:37.897 --> 00:03:39.847
That would be another model comparison,

00:03:40.277 --> 00:03:43.867
while this baseline keeps its
original forty eight pair definition.

